// The knowledge-graph corpus: four vertex types, five edge types, three of them cross-type.
//
// Everything this benchmark has measured so far — the flat 135–145k edges per window, the 1.0000×
// vertex over-read, the 4,096-row tile — comes from `bench.fossil`, which has **one** vertex type
// and **one** edge type. Two things in the writer have therefore never been exercised: `place_after`
// (`fossil-runtime/src/layout.rs`), which slides each type clear of the ones already placed, and the
// exclusion of cross-type edges from the layout — `VertexLayoutTarget::self_edge_csr` takes only the
// adjacencies whose `src_type` and `dst_type` are the same, so an edge between two types pulls on
// nothing and the communities on either side of it are computed in ignorance of each other.
//
// The shape is a citation graph, which is the ordinary knowledge-graph shape: a large type with a
// dense self-edge (`Paper` / `cites`), a second large one with a sparser self-edge
// (`Author` / `coauthor`), and two small types with **no** self-edge at all (`Venue`, `Topic`) —
// the case where the layout has no topology to lay out and falls back to one community per vertex.
//
// **The cross-type edges are community-aligned on purpose.** A paper's authors, venue and topics
// are drawn from the band of the other type that corresponds to the paper's own community, so the
// edges are as semantically local as a generator can make them. If they still come out long, that is
// the placement and not the data.
//
// **`in` and `on` are keywords, and the failure does not say so.** `kg:in = ...` parses without a
// complaint from `fossil check` and dies inside the writer with `Projections require unique
// expression names but the expression "?table?.community AS community" at position 1 and ... at
// position 2 have the same name` — a DataFusion planning error naming a column that has nothing to
// do with it. Renaming the property to `kg:publishedIn` is the whole fix. Recorded here because it
// cost four bisections of this file to find, and nothing in the message points at the property name.
//
// Two mappings of one vertex type are combined with a plain UNION ALL before the dedup by subject,
// so **every mapping of a type must project the same columns**. A vertex projection is
// `subject, <props…>, x, y, cluster_id` and an IRI-valued property is an edge rather than a prop, so
// each `Paper` block below carries `kg:community` and costs one column whatever edge it mints.
// `fossil check` accepts a mismatched pair; only `fossil run` rejects it.

prefix kg: <https://kanzo.tech/kg/>

papers   := io.csv("kg/papers.csv")
authors  := io.csv("kg/authors.csv")
venues   := io.csv("kg/venues.csv")
topics   := io.csv("kg/topics.csv")
cites    := io.csv("kg/cites.csv")
coauthor := io.csv("kg/coauthor.csv")
wrote    := io.csv("kg/wrote.csv")
appeared := io.csv("kg/appeared.csv")
about    := io.csv("kg/about.csv")

Paper : kg:Paper from papers
    iri = `${kg:}p/${.id}`
    kg:community = .community

Paper : kg:Paper from cites
    iri = `${kg:}p/${.id}`
    kg:community = .community
    kg:cites = `${kg:}p/${.target}`

Paper : kg:Paper from wrote
    iri = `${kg:}p/${.id}`
    kg:community = .community
    kg:authoredBy = `${kg:}a/${.target}`

Paper : kg:Paper from appeared
    iri = `${kg:}p/${.id}`
    kg:community = .community
    kg:publishedIn = `${kg:}v/${.target}`

Paper : kg:Paper from about
    iri = `${kg:}p/${.id}`
    kg:community = .community
    kg:hasTopic = `${kg:}t/${.target}`

Author : kg:Author from authors
    iri = `${kg:}a/${.id}`
    kg:community = .community

Author : kg:Author from coauthor
    iri = `${kg:}a/${.id}`
    kg:community = .community
    kg:coauthoredWith = `${kg:}a/${.target}`

Venue : kg:Venue from venues
    iri = `${kg:}v/${.id}`
    kg:community = .community

Topic : kg:Topic from topics
    iri = `${kg:}t/${.id}`
    kg:community = .community
